 art requires something the  eye cannot describe an atmosphere of  artistic theory and knowledge of history  of art an art world and then diggie says  well this needs some elucidation what  are we talking about here  not on the exhibited properties complex  properties rather than simple properties  he says that is absolutely central and  he says in speaking of atmosphere in  history Don Jose remark carries us a  step further he points towards the rich  structure in which particular works of  art are embedded he indicates the  institutional nature of arms and this is  what Dicky thinks is is a really  significant contribution so he begins in  this essay what is art by talking about  whether this exists or not and he brings  up the case of George Bernard Shaw who  uses this metaphor of an apostolic line  of succession stretching from Excellus  to himself  Ashlin is one of the earliest Greek  tragedy in so one of the great  contributors to early drama George  Bernard Shaw is writing from the vantage  point of his own presence now our past  and he's saying there's this line in  apostolic succession  what is apostolic succession well that's  a theological notion that's being used  metaphorically here and it means that he  can trace himself back to someone who  can trace himself back to trace himself  back and it goes all the way back to the  originators presumably the Apostles in  this case the nationalists Sophocles  Euripides you know the ancient Greek  playwrights maybe with Aristophanes  thrown in there as well but your  strasser you liked his comedy as well  and the idea is that there's some sort  of continuity of the art form being  carried up we can talk about you know  philosophical schools having had  successions you know think about the  early plainness and Aristotelians and  Epicureans and the Stoics they had their  school arcs the people who were in  charge you know promulgating the  teachings and running the school and  then you know people said I learned from  so-and-so I learned from so-and-so now  in the case of Shaw of course he's not  actually like learning from people who  learned all the way back it's it's  rather reading and participating in in  institutions that way so if we look at  theater though Dickey says that the  tradition may have run thin at times and  perhaps even cease to exist altogether  during some periods but it was reborn  out of memory and the need for art the  institution was associated with the  theater theater and varied from time to  time in the beginning it was Greek  religion in the great state and medieval  times the church more recently private  business of the state national theater  but what is remain constant with its own  identity is the theater itself with an  established way of doing and behaving  primary conventions and so you know we  can say the same thing not only about  theater he says theater is only one of  the systems within the art world each of  the systems has its own origins and  historical development and we might say  that we actually have more systems now  then perhaps Dickey did at the time of  his writing and Dickey perhaps more at  the time of his writing that in the time  of George Bernard Shaw I think about how  important film had become by the time  that Dickey is writing these these works  so he says that one central feature that  all these systems have  common is that each of them is a  framework for presenting of particular  works of art saying this is art take a  look at preferring are bringing it  forward for whatever it's going to be  the spectator the appreciator the  listener the reader to to appreciate and  to note as being arms what they don't  bring forward presumably then is not art  or at least it's not art that they're  bringing forward perhaps they're relying  on somebody else to do that instead so  this is a very important idea the art  world is a whole bunch of systems that  are connected together and each of them  furnishes an institutional 